  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the restaurant dining room, Chef Barinov walks in on Elena chatting warmly with Hotel Owner Eleonora. To his horror, the two women are bonding over their terrible first husbands without Elena realizing Victor is Eleonora's ex.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Доброе утро!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вы тут общаетесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Почему ты ничего мне не рассказывал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, я собирался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "У тебя такая замечательная начальница.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Я узнала о тебе много нового.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Как о подчинённом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Мы, оказывается, подруги по несчастью.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "У Элеоноры был неудачный брак.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В разводе всегда виноваты двое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Даже не знаешь человека и защищаешь его.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Работа не ждёт, пора бежать.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Доброе утро!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning!' Notice how Russian expresses 'good' and 'morning' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вы тут общаетесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you two chatting here?' The meaning hinges on 'and' working together with 'are chatting' in spoken Russian. Elena answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Почему ты ничего мне не рассказывал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why did you not tell me anything?' Elena brings out the contrast between 'why' and 'you' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, я собирался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, I was going to.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'come on' before landing on 'to intend'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "У тебя такая замечательная начальница.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You have such a wonderful boss.' Notice how Russian expresses 'at' and 'female boss' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Я узнала о тебе много нового.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I learned a lot of new things about you.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'new'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Как о подчинённом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'As a subordinate.' In Russian, the idea of 'how' flows naturally into 'subordinate' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Мы, оказывается, подруги по несчастью.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It turns out we are friends in misfortune.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'we' and finishing on 'misfortune'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "У Элеоноры был неудачный брак.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Eleonora had an unhappy marriage.' Elena anchors the line on 'at', while 'marriage' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В разводе всегда виноваты двое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In a divorce, two people are always at fault.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'divorce' before landing on 'two people'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Даже не знаешь человека и защищаешь его.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You do not even know the man and you defend him.' Notice how Russian expresses 'even' and 'his' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Elena answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Работа не ждёт, пора бежать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Work does not wait, it is time to run.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'work' before landing on 'to run'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
